[{"id":"VRCE-4-59-1","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":1,"english":"When the vulture had offered oblations of water to the manes of his deceased brother and finished his ablutions, the monkey-chiefs sat down in that charming mountain, surrounding him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.644,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-1"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059001","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7f400bd0d3dbbc411ae294d343c2f8d7d5ea716987b133557a9f7f1e2d38bee5","compact":"dcfb41fcc43cdad95ce1e1e21d35bb60d5b39b3f81fb16d86c724350078d0381","nasal":"183270b874363d09dc566aa29afce3bec5849be713d613570dd08c323a41c22d"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-2","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":2,"english":"And inspired with confidence in the words of the Nisakara), Sampati, from delight, said to Angada, who was seated, surrounded by all the monkeys.'","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-2"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059002","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"a0cee2ff5413c157b013615dc862469c4c822c5f3e337d02cdf7b7ec60d5399b","compact":"bb5c71470e220e747fd5fa9cc709330b5ef95cd2969ec99c882584ea4cabcdd7","nasal":"6d5b825e43436bd5f79a8bcb17e19d518e1b8624441b57f0ced11cdf9fb90329"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-3","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":3,"english":"Remaining silent, do you, you monkeys, heedfully hear what I say. I will tell you truly how I came to know Maithili.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.826,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-3"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059003","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bb367c147d7b0bcb089190ccfeb87ee38c1dd816349345bfd05fed1167444531","compact":"e37222ae339126ef4296716eb9cc161278f030f7471782b0b3af0e3154052687","nasal":"23cbf2636ec62e2003878beadebb1c015bd25f959d8192297b8d3a23d0c26a61"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-4","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":4,"english":"My body being affected by solar heat, and my wings burnt by the rays of the sun, O sinless one, I formerly fell to this summit of Vindhya.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.746,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-4"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059004","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c5a1773b1e0d89ac6abd26f70fe3592aedf2a551fe0a92619dacde4d7a2aa879","compact":"19144ec9e997160953bda7a9b6e9d9d133c30b92451060e2a1668db672e969b5","nasal":"7fbbcc767c9bc4c09b0008145f460a87d216dcb1daf461bf1b29addcc975ba68"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-5","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":5,"english":"On regaining my senses after six nights, during which I was insensible, I looked around in bewilderment, but could not recognise anything whatever.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.727,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-5"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059005","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"fe3197ed67ce2c02fac01e6567e3d694cc01f9d4696fecde5d58551a113f6c9c","compact":"8595e3112244d6d7e490cbe6c5e00d8b5e17dedeeb369451b3922fc273cf96eb","nasal":"e341e86ad262ad1503a9f0b2a4d91158b91375915017647bfc8b7d570d51ef46"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-6","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":6,"english":"Then closely viewing oceans and mountains, rivers and tanks, and woods and countries, my thoughts came back to me.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.476,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-6"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059006","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"efb27955a4b3db95088071e440c1e25b67852962e4adb2c4640655b607c0960c","compact":"019dbdcaabbd53ccd0dd1f888d2e6b2a970f1bc17b071b0278494074cf6e1574","nasal":"83e61052d97280ae80ee2f33389b7a19c55cc98d59469d8ceb55de3a6129c609"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-7","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":7,"english":"Seeing this place abounding in cheerful birds, containing woods and crested with summits, I for certain concluded it to be Vindhya on the shores of the Southern sea.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.841,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-7"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059007","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"162e03840e178331e22ea2a81e8182afa54f603affa7275ad8857e0d7dd20981","compact":"5fb025fb7a5d8b854672e2b2aeaff8b31ebc0365632cbe5ae47aba20fef3997b","nasal":"df389696cfdc559846c1a5472996c52b396f37092a11511700bdde460ee17f6f"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-8","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":8,"english":"And here lay a sacred asylum, honoured even by the celestials, and here dwelt a saint named Niśākara, of fierce austerities.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059008","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6bcc59a81db39134bbefeef20d7ea924bf379696bc5944ad3893292772f8fb9f","compact":"72ec9b13293a8d99c5dca15542fd90840a0cdcf08fb3876d8c23b7ebdf26d0f9","nasal":"9e08331b1bacced6ba8a5fa4179228b788f44f6f4c6cfe07c9d18aafd4f83efd"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-9","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":9,"english":"After Niśākara cognizant of virtue had gone to heaven, I bereft of that saint, passed eight thousand years in this mountain.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.677,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-9"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059009","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"7f5eed88e8152c9608bab62b0fdfa8db7718a638f7c1311a45a72511d82e5957","compact":"d6384f1233d15a35bee45c02ec7ab36e49d3bc9cf3ce5bb8d351adb96318f4ab","nasal":"cb36675b115447969f37b59999b700d4400ef8552fbe489da6f8821190d8f804"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-10","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":10,"english":"Descending with difficulty from the uneven top of Vindhya, I again came* to the earth covered with sharp-pointed grass. *While the saint was staying here.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.783,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059010","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"e47f8aec4c67e63110cd9568cb5fca940e5ac751ddb67b0f22a9720ff3afd9dc","compact":"0eae4258652e96982f153057949d6fe7778e8c9535ff5f3069ccf815a093a1b7","nasal":"7e24a97a13206f49e4003a15c5dc096d5cfb1e4eaf92df11e0dd89a8935a5ac8"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-11","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":11,"english":"I was eager to see the saint; and (therefore), with much ado I came to him; and Jatāyu and myself saw that saint many a time and oft.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.867,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059011","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"674f16e135b6ea4f825c8ceae6176d96c6acb788d30f2958951389f43e579668","compact":"31885e888590b1a99f612729ba9e497efbd0d84dd8a369fddd576389cfbb89f3","nasal":"31885e888590b1a99f612729ba9e497efbd0d84dd8a369fddd576389cfbb89f3"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-12","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":12,"english":"A fragrant breeze blew about the hermitage; and no tree was found that did not bear flowers and fruits.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059012","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"f0b09a6f18509b5cc76999ce2911235b7bc5433dcb8d8276525bb2d31e6ce545","compact":"8a095337b927e1a2bd9ad781a3e579b1e5c562e2156af3b061fca7260270ff3c","nasal":"c5db3769a29a4eb648303d9bca012d25f070639e1b996aa36bfb97f8f25795e3"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-13","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":13,"english":"Approaching the holy asylum, I, desirous of beholding the worshipful Niśākara, waited at the foot of a tree.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.879,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059013","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"90d2a4ce7c0bb86aa5c05e7fa1a0d5b309c2fc2a6e36e2051051d785f2e7b7f9","compact":"807ed818cdcfb6f0097f98a94ac991de33ca34b81b37392d5a776a0827a2c314","nasal":"e90c5b63925dbaf0f5eccfdd8516eb4399a5672713846e61488ece1b238bb85e"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-14","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":14,"english":"Then at a distance I saw the saint, flaming in energy and irrepressible,-returning northwards.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.6,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059014","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6197b435d39976f3d5460e449842a774c3b1a326ee6a4d12c2f887ac1c5c9605","compact":"0afb4b312efd0c45abf87e7bff39b84e82a6884e8dec859b276a8e97bab8243b","nasal":"0afb4b312efd0c45abf87e7bff39b84e82a6884e8dec859b276a8e97bab8243b"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-15","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":15,"english":"As creatures gather round a giver, bears and Srimaras, tigers lions, and various reptiles, were going along, surrounding him.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.853,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059015","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"6497f64ef52c0a353ad491e9515a80dd1f7343653e52e46514618083160b3f92","compact":"162eae34a66c1c5e8ab40a5695107c65b61a2fdd6bed58732d3897e10fbd24aa","nasal":"d0c0f67d12c9585b79a69a83601bc0aa708b0bde71d85a59b0af0bbf836670ef"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-16","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":16,"english":"And knowing that the saint had arrived (at his home), the animals went away, as go away forces together with counsellors, when the sovereign has entered (his palace).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.886,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059016","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"b6aca32c98cc44d6c7f336c99ff2abda9dd02de141f8dee2f73c2fa0ca0bce1f","compact":"80947df194e1df6da8dbcabc9f5b6c41686cd60e746f3b7eaa645861915acedd","nasal":"80947df194e1df6da8dbcabc9f5b6c41686cd60e746f3b7eaa645861915acedd"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-17","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":17,"english":"The saint was glad on seeing me. Then reentering his asylum for a short space, he came out and enquired for my mission.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.808,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059017","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0895acb204cd95318f6260c721096edc4dfb4d72a72c76a0df265469f6b9c9cc","compact":"a78de24db2038f07dcec2e609690ebd31e46fe83a79c672d5b1b4a198f92e4a4","nasal":"288c337634848631021c298565edbd2fbb59d83c9af4c6e9d81d738cbad3f683"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-18","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":18,"english":"O mild one, in consequence of your feathers having undergone alteration, I cannot recognise you. Your two wings have been blasted by fire; and your powers in your frail frame are in the same condition).","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.632,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059018","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1321e35061332caf261adaa8d0e1763a14b5520b7600521f6f9a82d595900ec8","compact":"1f8f4205cf589b574435e3f38931a2f0aeef6274579579e13436c0755734293c","nasal":"1f8f4205cf589b574435e3f38931a2f0aeef6274579579e13436c0755734293c"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-19","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":19,"english":"After time I saw two vultures like to wind in speed,-kings of vultures, brothers, wearing shapes at will.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.734,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-19"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059019","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"181462262dd17cf361772357f45389e397011155b305b99d9d16b845860d2077","compact":"17747caead499a6d4b2702ea6eb337dae17db7ad4c8e12aaa6a4b84072d2cdff","nasal":"17747caead499a6d4b2702ea6eb337dae17db7ad4c8e12aaa6a4b84072d2cdff"}},{"id":"VRCE-4-59-20","kanda":4,"kandaId":"kishkindha","sarga":59,"verse":20,"english":"You are I know, elder, O Sampati; and Jatayu is younger to you. Assuming human forms, you had taken hold of my feet.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.607,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-4-60-20"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"4059020","sanskritFile":"Ram04.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9e9f5bb8aa5374f13a038911b7d590fd833b8be892e0eb